1
00:00:00,905 --> 00:00:02,250
This is sotto:

2
00:00:02,379 --> 00:00:05,988
confidential, budgeted, auditable payments for AI agents.

3
00:00:06,847 --> 00:00:10,874
Today, every x402 payment an agent makes is public:

4
00:00:11,061 --> 00:00:14,461
what it bought, from whom, and for how much.

5
00:00:14,742 --> 00:00:17,004
Its strategy is readable on-chain —

6
00:00:17,075 --> 00:00:20,336
and nothing caps what it can spend.

7
00:00:21,799 --> 00:00:23,532
Here's a paid API.

8
00:00:23,552 --> 00:00:27,642
It answers 402 with both kinds of challenge in one response —

9
00:00:27,662 --> 00:00:30,881
x402, and the Machine Payments Protocol —

10
00:00:30,901 --> 00:00:32,414
offering three rails:

11
00:00:32,434 --> 00:00:37,749
Solana confidential transfers, shielded Zcash, and Tempo.

12
00:00:38,969 --> 00:00:41,092
Meet Capy, our demo agent.

13
00:00:41,454 --> 00:00:43,286
Its owner set a policy:

14
00:00:43,648 --> 00:00:47,605
at most fifty cents a payment, a dollar fifty a day.

15
00:00:48,071 --> 00:00:50,547
Each quote costs twenty-five cents,

16
00:00:50,567 --> 00:00:55,569
and settles as a Solana confidential transfer in under two seconds.

17
00:00:57,846 --> 00:01:00,990
Six quotes in, the budget is spent —

18
00:01:01,271 --> 00:01:04,973
and the seventh is refused before anything is signed.

19
00:01:06,527 --> 00:01:08,836
Here are the same payments, three ways.

20
00:01:08,965 --> 00:01:10,826
The public sees the transactions,

21
00:01:10,846 --> 00:01:12,649
but every amount is encrypted,

22
00:01:12,669 --> 00:01:16,069
and the API's public balance reads zero.

23
00:01:17,181 --> 00:01:18,886
The owner sees everything:

24
00:01:19,073 --> 00:01:23,843
a signed receipt per payment, with the policy decision behind it.

25
00:01:24,906 --> 00:01:27,649
And an auditor with the mint's auditor key

26
00:01:27,669 --> 00:01:30,616
decrypts the exact amount from the ledger itself.

27
00:01:30,897 --> 00:01:33,043
The proofs are found on-chain;

28
00:01:33,416 --> 00:01:36,491
nothing is taken on the agent's word.

29
00:01:38,291 --> 00:01:39,984
Any agent can plug in.

30
00:01:40,601 --> 00:01:43,925
This is Claude Code with the sotto MCP server,

31
00:01:43,945 --> 00:01:47,090
asked to buy a one-dollar research report.

32
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:55,231
It paid confidentially, and it quotes its receipt.

33
00:01:55,790 --> 00:01:58,812
Agents with a shell get the same tools as an Agent Skill,

34
00:01:58,832 --> 00:02:01,222
and every door shares one kill switch:

35
00:02:01,351 --> 00:02:04,728
pause, and the next payment is refused.

36
00:02:07,597 --> 00:02:12,448
The same policy runs on Tempo, over its native Machine Payments Protocol:

37
00:02:12,636 --> 00:02:16,813
twenty-five cents, paid on the Moderato testnet —

38
00:02:17,777 --> 00:02:21,978
and there it is, on Tempo's public explorer.

39
00:02:25,795 --> 00:02:28,719
On Zcash, the payment is fully shielded.

40
00:02:29,092 --> 00:02:31,603
The API finds it with a viewing key,

41
00:02:31,623 --> 00:02:35,197
by the payment ID in the encrypted memo.

42
00:02:42,005 --> 00:02:45,080
One owner, one ledger, three chains.

43
00:02:45,209 --> 00:02:48,435
sotto: confidential, not anonymous.

44
00:02:48,623 --> 00:02:51,221
Open source, and ready for any agent.
